/-!
# Independence of polynomial presentation for locally Koszul-regular kernels

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07CZ
(lemma-koszul-independence-presentation), with the local condition of Tag 07CV.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-koszul-polynomial-presentation-independence).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace Algebra

/-- Local Koszul regularity of a kernel is independent of the finite polynomial
presentation. The local condition is expanded at each prime containing the kernel.
Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07CZ, using Tag 07CV(2).
(blueprint: lem-sfb-koszul-polynomial-presentation-independence).

Proof (the node's primewise refinement, without global finite generation):
1. Put P = R[x], Q = R[y] and C = R[x,y]. Lift the images of y to polynomials
f in P and the images of x to polynomials g in Q. The evaluations e_y and e_x
have common composite gamma to S. Subtracting the evaluated polynomial monomial
by monomial gives K = ker gamma = (I,y-f) = (J,x-g), with I = ker alpha and
J = ker beta. It also gives C/(y-f) = P and C/(x-g) = Q.
2. At a prime q of S, choose u outside its inverse image in P and a Koszul-regular
sequence a generating I_u. The graph sequence d = y-f is weakly regular in C_u:
translation identifies its successive quotients with polynomial rings where a
variable acts injectively. Apply lem-weil-pbc-regular-sequence-koszul-regular and
lem-sfb-koszul-prefix-quotient-criterion to obtain the Koszul-regular sequence
(d,a) generating K_u, of length m+t.
3. Put v = e_x(u), outside the inverse image of q in Q. Evaluation C_u onto Q_v
sends K_u onto J_v: lift a numerator of an element of J_v and use the common
composite gamma. Thus the images of (d,a) give finitely many generators of J_v.
4. The conormal-basis theorem lem-sc-koszul-conormal-basis gives dimension t for
the I-conormal fibre over the residue field kappa at q. Let s be the finite
J-conormal fibre dimension. Apply lem-weil-pbc-conormal-module-principal-localization
at the element 1, and tensor its stable isomorphism with kappa, to get t+m=s+n.
These fibres equal I tensor_P kappa and J tensor_Q kappa because the squares
act as zero; localization at u or v does not change the fibres.
5. Choose b in J lifting a basis of its conormal fibre, clearing powers of v
from a basis selected among the finite generators. Apply
lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-local-generators at the inverse-image prime in Q. Clear
finitely many coefficient denominators and equality errors for the generators
of J_v to obtain w outside that prime with J_(vw) = (b). This includes s=0.
6. In D = C_(uvw), flat localization preserves the regularity of (d,a), by
lem-weil-pbc-localization-flat and lem-sc-koszul-regular-flat-base-change.
The same ideal KD is generated by (x-g,b), of equal length n+s=m+t.
Apply lem-sfb-koszul-equal-length-generators to make this sequence Koszul-regular.
7. Translation makes the prefix x-g weakly regular already in C, hence
Koszul-regular by the weak-regular theorem and still so in D by flat base change.
The reverse implication of lem-sfb-koszul-prefix-quotient-criterion makes b
Koszul-regular in D/(x-g).
8. Evaluation identifies this quotient with Q_(v²w), canonically Q_(vw),
where b generates J_(vw). Since vw avoids the chosen prime, this is the required
principal neighbourhood. Every prime containing J comes from a prime of S.
For S=0 there are no such primes. Empty variable sets and sequences obey the
same formulas; no flatness, Noetherianity or nontriviality assumption is needed. -/
theorem koszulRegular_kernel_of_polynomial_presentation
    {R : Type u} {S : Type v} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S]
    (n m : ℕ)
    (α : MvPolynomial (Fin n) R →ₐ[R] S) (hα : Function.Surjective α)
    (β : MvPolynomial (Fin m) R →ₐ[R] S) (hβ : Function.Surjective β)
    (hlocal : ∀ p : Ideal (MvPolynomial (Fin n) R), p.IsPrime →
      RingHom.ker α.toRingHom ≤ p →
      ∃ u : MvPolynomial (Fin n) R, u ∉ p ∧
        ∃ (r : ℕ) (a : Fin r → Localization.Away u),
          (RingHom.ker α.toRingHom).map
              (algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin n) R) (Localization.Away u)) =
            Ideal.span (Set.range a) ∧ RingTheory.Sequence.IsKoszulRegular a) :
    ∀ p : Ideal (MvPolynomial (Fin m) R), p.IsPrime →
      RingHom.ker β.toRingHom ≤ p →
      ∃ u : MvPolynomial (Fin m) R, u ∉ p ∧
        ∃ (r : ℕ) (a : Fin r → Localization.Away u),
          (RingHom.ker β.toRingHom).map
              (algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin m) R) (Localization.Away u)) =
            Ideal.span (Set.range a) ∧ RingTheory.Sequence.IsKoszulRegular a := by
  sorry

end Algebra
